    <h2>The 30-minute test</h2>
    <p>Run 30 minutes all-out, as evenly as you can hold it. Three numbers come out of it: <strong>average pace</strong> over the 30 minutes is your threshold pace, <strong>average heart rate over the last 20 minutes</strong> is your LTHR, and — if you run with a power meter — <strong>average power</strong> over the 30 minutes is your threshold power.</p>
    <p>Early, rested, fed and hydrated. A flat uninterrupted loop: no traffic lights, no crossings, because every stop invalidates the average. Chest strap, always. If you do have to stop, the test isn't rescued by estimating — it can be recovered by calculating over the last 10 clean minutes, or repeated the following weekend with the cause fixed.</p>

    <h2>Three metrics, one order of trust</h2>
    <p>If you have more than one, the order is <strong>power &gt; pace &gt; heart rate</strong>. Power measures the work directly. Pace is excellent on the flat and a liar uphill, into wind, or on trail. Heart rate reacts late and drifts with heat, sleep and caffeine.</p>
    <p>That's about which one to <em>believe</em> when they disagree, not about which to use: the calculator shows you every metric you measured, because in practice you'll look at one or the other depending on the day and the terrain.</p>

    <h2>About running power</h2>
    <p>If you run with a power meter, these are power zones for the seven-zone 80/20 model — not the zones your meter's own software ships with, which usually uses five and splits them differently. The numbers are not interchangeable: use these with this model and the sessions in your plan will land where they're meant to.</p>

    <h2>The marathon-pace trap</h2>
    <p>Look at Zone X once you have the table. It's the top half of Zone 2, near enough your marathon pace, and it's separated on purpose: it accumulates a lot more fatigue than low Z2 without giving much more aerobic benefit in return.</p>
    <p>It's where most people overtrain without noticing. You head out easy, you feel good, the pace settles a few seconds quicker on its own, and you end up spending three sessions a week there. It feels like training. You arrive at your quality day with nothing in your legs.</p>
  </div>
</section>
